WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.860
Child actors don't just appear in kids'&nbsp;
movies. Plenty of actors in their teens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.660
and younger have been in films with gruesome&nbsp;
imagery or disturbing subject matter. And even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.221
if the filmmakers tried to shield them from&nbsp;
seeing too much, it surely had an effect.

00:00:14.221 --> 00:00:17.580
The first thing 12-year-old Tim does in&nbsp;
"River's Edge" is throw his sister's doll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.620
into a river. It's a mean-spirited act, but&nbsp;
it perfectly underscores the worldview of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.260
small-town teens in director Tim Hunter's 1986&nbsp;
film. Their alienation manifests in ways from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.700
theft and drug use to cold-blooded killing.&nbsp;
Tim is the youngest of the film's characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.935
but also the most casually amoral. By the&nbsp;
end, Tim threatens to kill his own brother.

00:00:38.492 --> 00:00:39.666
"You shouldn't have hit me."

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.440
"Don't be stupid, Tim."

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.120
Joshua John Miller, the son of playwright&nbsp;
and actor Jason Miller, was just 12 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.520
old when he replaced Corey Haim as Tim in "River's&nbsp;
Edge," and with only a handful of minor TV credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.780
to his name. But he's totally convincing&nbsp;
in carrying out Tim's list of cruelties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.180
which include stealing drug dealer Feck's gun and&nbsp;
later assaulting him with it. Miller would play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.260
other kids on the edge — most notably&nbsp;
in "Near Dark" — before transitioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.666
to his current career as a writer and TV&nbsp;
producer. But none were as chilling as Tim.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.920
The catalog of cruelties perpetrated by&nbsp;
Leena Klammer, a psychotic 33-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.420
posing as an adolescent girl, isn't the&nbsp;
most shocking element of 2009's "Orphan."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.480
Leena kills multiple people, threatens other&nbsp;
children, and attempts to seduce her adoptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.400
father, but the really disturbing aspect&nbsp;
of these incidents is that actress Isabelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.260
Fuhrman was only 12 years old when the movie&nbsp;
was made. Yet Fuhrman was more than happy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.334
explore the gory aspects of her character.&nbsp;
As she told the Boston Herald in 2009,

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:44.811
"If America hates me, I've done my job."

00:01:44.811 --> 00:01:49.020
Fuhrman doubled down on her astonishing&nbsp;
performance in "Orphan: First Kill," a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.300
prequel released nine years after the previous&nbsp;
film. Though 25 at the time, Fuhrman gave another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.760
chillingly believable turn as the 9-year-old Leena&nbsp;
with the help of body doubles, forced perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.940
and makeup special effects, but no CGI. Director&nbsp;
William Brent Bell told Showbiz Cheatsheet,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.868
"Sometimes I think the visual effects component of&nbsp;
[digital effects] actually makes it look worse."

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:15.420
In "Thirteen," Tracy Freeland, played by Evan&nbsp;
Rachel Wood, is already close to emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.460
ground zero when she enters the seventh grade. Her&nbsp;
divorced mom, Melanie, is a recovering alcoholic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:25.380
and her body image problems have led to&nbsp;
self-harming. When she meets popular girl Evie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.420
played by co-writer Nikki Reed, her need for&nbsp;
acceptance pushes her into rash acts, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:37.500
shoplifting, drug use, and casual sex, that spiral&nbsp;
into self-destructive behavior. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.357
Tracy has only her fraught relationship&nbsp;
with her mother as a support system.

00:02:41.357 --> 00:02:45.540
Director Catherine Hardwicke and Reed, who&nbsp;
later collaborated on the "Twilight" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.400
drew on Reed's own experiences when writing&nbsp;
"Thirteen." Though Reed later admitted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.020
she exaggerated some of her experiences in the&nbsp;
script, the anxiety and confusion of being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.880
teen girl rings true in much of the film. Wood&nbsp;
and Reed, who were 16 and 15, respectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:05.040
at the time of filming, were strictly monitored&nbsp;
by Hardwicke, their own mothers, and a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.540
welfare worker during their most difficult&nbsp;
scenes to ensure their emotional well being.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.100
In a 2015 interview with the late Anne&nbsp;
Heche and Heather Duffy for their podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.480
"Better Together with Anne &amp; Heather,"&nbsp;
actress Mariel Hemingway said audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.440
would never see her Oscar-nominated&nbsp;
breakout role in Woody Allen's 1979&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.021
comedy "Manhattan" if the movie was&nbsp;
released in the current film market.

00:03:26.021 --> 00:03:32.178
"I'm not condoning any behavior. I'm talking&nbsp;
about that movie. But that movie probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.545
couldn't come out today."

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:38.760
Why? For one, the film features a romantic&nbsp;
relationship between a 42-year-old writer, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.680
by Allen, and Hemingway's 17-year-old student.&nbsp;
While that scenario might have been offbeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.501
but acceptable in 1979, today, it reads as an&nbsp;
older man's creepy manipulation of a young girl.

00:03:49.501 --> 00:03:53.580
Another reason is the accusations by&nbsp;
Allen's adoptive daughter, Dylan Farrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.180
who alleged that he sexually abused&nbsp;
her when she was just 7 years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.260
It's very difficult to keep that out of&nbsp;
mind while watching Allen canoodle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.740
the teenaged Hemingway. But even the&nbsp;
most forgiving viewer must note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:10.234
the cultural landscape has changed and that an&nbsp;
adult with a teenager under 18 is inappropriate.

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:13.260
Femme fatales have been a Hollywood&nbsp;
trope since the days of silent films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.280
but the stereotype takes an exploitative&nbsp;
turn when the dangerous woman in question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.460
is a teenager. The 1992 movie "Poison Ivy"&nbsp;
aimed for a cross between film noir and "Fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.680
Attraction" in its story of a sexually free&nbsp;
but homicidally inclined teen. The casting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.300
Drew Barrymore — then in a career rebound after&nbsp;
struggles with drug addiction — and name players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.244
like Sara Gilbert and Tom Skerritt attempted&nbsp;
to lend respectability to the sleazy plot.

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:44.460
Barrymore, then 17, isn't particularly convincing&nbsp;
at depicting Ivy, a deranged teen so taken with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.780
Gilbert's normal life that she's willing to&nbsp;
seduce her father and kill her mother. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.980
character is aware of her desirability but&nbsp;
unable to use it for anything except base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:58.680
interests. Barrymore gamely handles the story's&nbsp;
more prurient aspects, which include making out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.360
with the 17-year-old Gilbert and 60-year-old&nbsp;
Skerritt and pushing Cheryl Ladd off a balcony.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.120
The 1998 film adaptation of Stephen King's novella&nbsp;
"Apt Pupil" differs from its source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.980
but the film retains its disturbing portrait&nbsp;
of evil's corrupting influence. The late Brad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.520
Renfro stars as Todd, a teenager who&nbsp;
discovers that his elderly neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.740
Kurt Dussander, played by Ian McKellen, is a&nbsp;
former Nazi. The teen blackmails Dussander into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.240
telling grisly stories from concentration&nbsp;
camps. Their relationship warps both men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.844
in dangerous ways — Todd blackmails his guidance&nbsp;
counselor, while Kurt starts killing transients.

00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:36.540
Though some of the grislier elements of&nbsp;
King's story are toned down for the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.120
many are featured in Brandon Boyce's script. For&nbsp;
Renfro, who was 17 years old during production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.200
that meant participating in scenes where&nbsp;
Todd helped kill a homeless man and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.460
fantasy sequence in which he envisions his&nbsp;
classmates as concentration camp prisoners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.660
which prompted a lawsuit against director&nbsp;
Bryan Singer for infliction of emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.700
distress and other charges. Todd's corrupted&nbsp;
innocence was familiar to Renfro throughout his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.380
acting career in films including "The Client"&nbsp;
and, tragically, in his own life off-camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.660
which ended with his 2008 death from&nbsp;
a drug overdose at the age of 25.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.040
Petr Kotlar was just 11 years old when he&nbsp;
was cast in Czech director Vaclav Marhoul's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.020
2019 adaptation of Jerzy Kosinski's novel "The&nbsp;
Painted Bird." He couldn't have picked a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.060
harrowing story for his debut; the movie follows&nbsp;
a mostly silent boy wandering through a surreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.640
hellscape in Eastern Europe during the early days&nbsp;
of World War II. The boy is brutalized — beaten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.140
sexually assaulted, and tortured —&nbsp;
and witnesses horrific behavior by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:38.460
every adult he meets. Kotlar had never&nbsp;
acted on film before but holds his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.607
in scenes opposite Stellan Skarsgaard,&nbsp;
Harvey Keitel, and the late Julian Sands.

00:06:43.607 --> 00:06:47.580
In an interview with The Guardian, Marhoul&nbsp;
said he shielded Kotlar from the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:52.560
traumatizing scenes by capturing his reactions&nbsp;
separately from the action. He also relied on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.580
Kotlar's family and a full-time assistant to&nbsp;
provide him with emotional support when it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.980
needed. Despite the protection, the experience&nbsp;
seems to have impacted Kotlar. Marhoul said,

00:07:02.145 --> 00:07:05.205
"After, he told me he&nbsp;
doesn't want to be an actor."

00:07:05.205 --> 00:07:09.600
Alicia Silverstone made her big screen&nbsp;
debut in "The Crush," a supremely icky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.340
thriller that cast the 17-year-old actress as&nbsp;
a 14-year-old lethal Lolita with designs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:20.340
a 28-year-old journalist played by Cary Elwes.&nbsp;
The film, directed by TV vet Alan Shapiro, plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.540
on familiar sexist tropes about teenage girls&nbsp;
and the clueless adult men who fall for them.

00:07:24.912 --> 00:07:29.532
"I'll always be your friend,&nbsp;
no matter what. OK? OK."

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.580
"Like that night up at the&nbsp;
lighthouse when we kissed?"

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:35.880
Elwes' character steps into every snare laid by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.240
Silverstone and then experiences&nbsp;
a moment of crisis about her age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.800
which is supposed to generate sympathy when she&nbsp;
unleashes a torrent of psychotic behavior on him.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.540
"The Crush" portrays Silverstone as a&nbsp;
contemptible object while also fetishizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:53.588
her sexuality. As the film's villain, she's given&nbsp;
all sorts of outlandish schemes to carry out:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.588 --> 00:07:58.860
she nearly kills Elwes' new girlfriend and pins a&nbsp;
charge of sexual assault on him. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.360
the film also affirms that it's okay to enjoy&nbsp;
the camera ogling a supposed 14-year-old at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.400
every turn. The Washington Post's review summed up&nbsp;
the film's have-it-both-ways approach by noting,

00:08:08.772 --> 00:08:11.292
"There's something scuzzy&nbsp;
about the whole exercise."

00:08:11.292 --> 00:08:17.124
Guillermo del Toro is a passionate advocate of&nbsp;
writer-director Issa Lopez's 2017 Mexican film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:21.360
"Tigers Are Not Afraid," which is both&nbsp;
a haunting supernatural thriller and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.980
melancholy look at the impact of drug violence.&nbsp;
But as with del Toro's films like "The Devil's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.940
Backbone" and "Pan's Labyrinth," "Tigers&nbsp;
Are Not Afraid" is also about the terrors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.443
and traumas of childhood and how some children&nbsp;
learn to survive them while others do not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.443 --> 00:08:38.280
 To deliver that gut punch, Lopez worked&nbsp;
with an extraordinary group of young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.900
actors and put them through an emotional&nbsp;
workout, though it was carefully guided.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.160
The young actors in "Tigers Are Not Afraid"&nbsp;
play residents of an unnamed Mexican city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.960
ravaged by drug lords. The children lose&nbsp;
virtually everything — parents, homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.000
even mementos — and death is around them&nbsp;
at all times. Bodies lie in the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.860
adults and children are killed violently in front&nbsp;
of them, and children are forced to fight and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.200
kill in return. Ghosts are also a constant&nbsp;
presence, urging revenge for their deaths.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.640
Director Scott Derrickson's thriller "The Black&nbsp;
Phone" is so briskly paced that viewers might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.260
almost miss the storyline's truly horrible&nbsp;
elements. An adaptation of a short story by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:21.060
Joe Hill, the film focuses on Finney,&nbsp;
played by Mason Thames, a teenager who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.280
has been abducted by a child murderer known as&nbsp;
The Grabber. Though terrified, Finney begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.720
receive visions and messages from The Grabber's&nbsp;
deceased victims and formulates an escape plan.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.400
Finney's communication with the victims&nbsp;
is so otherworldly that it's almost easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.200
to forget these kids have died in particularly&nbsp;
tragic ways. They were stolen off the streets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:45.120
of their hometowns and tormented by a sadistic&nbsp;
masked killer who eventually took their lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:49.620
in a way that undoubtedly "really hurt," as&nbsp;
The Grabber puts it. The unendurable sadness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:53.580
of Finney's fate and their deaths is felt&nbsp;
in the emotional line readings by Thames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.020
who was 14 when the film was released&nbsp;
in 2021, and his young cast mates.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.880
In 2015's "Goodnight Mommy," a woman&nbsp;
returns to her isolated home after surgery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.140
where her twin sons greet her. At least, they&nbsp;
believe she's their mother, but her angry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.580
often violent reactions to them cause the&nbsp;
boys to suspect otherwise. They hold her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.780
captive in increasingly cruel ways to&nbsp;
extract a confession from her, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:20.366
they eventually discover is far more complicated&nbsp;
and chilling than a simple impostor situation.

00:10:20.366 --> 00:10:25.140
Adding considerably to the creepiness of this&nbsp;
Austrian film is that two young boys play the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:30.300
twins. Elias and Lukas Schwarz, who, according&nbsp;
to various sources, were 12 while filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:35.340
carry out many unsettling acts. They raise&nbsp;
huge cockroaches and look after a dead cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.240
and eventually tie down and seal their&nbsp;
alleged mother's mouth shut to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.400
her from screaming for help. When she refuses to&nbsp;
change her story, the boys glue her to the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.440
and set the house ablaze. By that point, it's&nbsp;
far too late for anyone to change their story.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:55.740
Michael Haneke's 1997 film "Funny Games" is a&nbsp;
polarizing experience. You're either wowed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:00.480
the movie's deliberate manipulation to produce&nbsp;
maximum horror or completely disgusted by its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:06.060
catalog of sadism. The brutality two strangers&nbsp;
bring upon an Austrian family is painful to watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:09.566
and the torment seems to have no&nbsp;
point beyond amusing the culprits.

00:11:09.566 --> 00:11:14.520
The family's son, Schorshie, suffers some of&nbsp;
the worst abuse. Not only does he witness his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:18.480
parents' torture, but he also stumbles upon the&nbsp;
corpse of his neighbors' daughter in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.640
to flee. The boy, played German actor Stefan&nbsp;
Clapczynski, who was only 11 at the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.560
filming, is also featured in one of the film's&nbsp;
most infamous scenes, when he's mercilessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.266
shot by one of the strangers. The on-screen&nbsp;
death of a child is upsetting for audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.266 --> 00:11:39.437
but when accompanied by so much other awful&nbsp;
behavior, it's an almost unbearable experience.

